  "pinShiftPaperExampleEncode": "Worked example for a 4-digit PIN, encode +3719:",
  "pinShiftPaperExampleEncodeNote": "The last column is 4 + 9 = 13: drop the tens digit.",
  "pinShiftPaperExampleDecode": "Worked example, decode −3719:",
  "pinShiftPaperExampleDecodeNote": "The last column is 3 − 9 = −6: add 10.",
  "pinShiftPaperRulesTitle": "Rules in one line",
  "pinShiftPaperRules": "• No carry between columns. Each position is independent.\n• Encode = add, decode = subtract. Currently: {direction}.\n• Sum ≥ 10: drop the leading 1.\n• Difference < 0: add 10.\n• Same arithmetic for 4-digit or 16-digit PINs.",
  "@pinShiftPaperRules": {
    "placeholders": {
      "direction": { "type": "String" }
    }
  },
  "pinShiftDirectionEncode": "encode",
  "pinShiftDirectionDecode": "decode",
  "pinShiftPaperDontTitle": "What not to do on paper",
  "pinShiftPaperDontBody": "• Don't keep base, vector and new on one sheet you carry around: keep the vector in your head and destroy the sheet right after.\n• Don't use a vector of all zeros — it changes nothing.\n• Don't use a vector equal to the base: new = 2 × base (mod 10) shows the pattern.\n• Don't reuse one vector for PIN families of different sensitivity: one leaked (base, new) pair breaks them all.",
  "pinShiftThreatTitle": "Threat model & limitations",
  "pinShiftThreatBody": "• Not a cipher. A Caesar-style additive shift with no cryptographic strength — use a password manager or a hardware key for real secrets.\n• The vector must be as long as the PIN ({length} here). Anything shorter would shrink the keyspace below 10^{length}.\n• Base PIN leaked, vector private: an attacker faces 10^{length} candidates for the derived PIN — the same as a random PIN.\n• One leaked (base, new) pair reveals the whole vector with one subtraction — fundamental to any additive shift.\n• Several new PINs without their bases leak the pairwise differences of the bases per position — dangerous if the bases are correlated (dates, phone numbers).\n• The algorithm is public: only the vector is secret.",
  "@pinShiftThreatBody": {
    "placeholders": {
      "length": { "type": "int" }
    }
  },
  "pinShiftUseTitle": "When it makes sense",
  "pinShiftUseBody": "• Low- to medium-value PINs that lock after a few wrong tries (cards, door locks, local apps).\n• You don't want to remember or write down a separate PIN for every card.\n• You are ready to change the base PINs if a (base, new) pair may have leaked.",
  "pinShiftDontUseTitle": "When not to use it",
  "pinShiftDontUseBody": "• Real secrets: bank passwords, seed phrases, private keys — use a password manager or a hardware key.\n• Anything without a retry limit: brute force is instant there.\n• When someone may know both a base and a new PIN (family, colleagues, former partners).",
  "pinLegacyBadge": "Legacy",
